---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import Command from '../components/Command.astro'
import ThemePicker from '../components/ThemePicker.vue'
import { themes } from '../data/catalog'

const bases = themes.filter((theme) => theme.kind === 'base')
const accents = themes.filter((theme) => theme.kind === 'accent')
---

<Layout title="Themes. Ermine UI" description="Colour themes for Ermine UI: a base that tints the greys and an accent for the primary colour, in light and dark.">
  <div class="wrap doc">
    <header class="doc-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Themes</p>
      <h1 class="headline">Pick a base and an accent.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A base tints the surfaces, text and lines; an accent sets the primary colour and the focus ring. Every pairing is
        checked for readable contrast in light and dark.
      </p>
    </header>

    <ThemePicker client:load themes={themes} />

    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="apply">Applying a theme</h2>
      <p>
        Name a base, an accent, or both. The command writes them at the end of your theme stylesheet, each in its own marked
        block, and replaces only that block when you pick again. Nothing else in the file changes.
      </p>
      <Command args={['theme slate blue', 'theme rose', 'theme --reset']} />
      <p>
        The second line keeps the slate base and swaps the accent; <code>--reset</code> removes both. To set one up from the
        start, use <code>init --theme slate,blue</code>. To adjust a colour, set the variable again in a rule after the theme's
        block. AI coding tools connected to <code>ermine mcp</code> can do the same with its <code>set_theme</code> tool.
      </p>

      <h2 id="bases">Bases</h2>
      <ul>{bases.map((theme) => <li><code>{theme.name}</code>: {theme.summary}</li>)}</ul>

      <h2 id="accents">Accents</h2>
      <ul>{accents.map((theme) => <li><code>{theme.name}</code>: {theme.summary}</li>)}</ul>
      <p>With no accent, the primary colour is the base's own near-black (near-white in the dark theme).</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
